namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page user_manual_page User manual

  This manual describes how to use %Ponca. It assumes that the library is installed, see
  \ref ponca_getting_started_page.

  \section user_manual_modules Modules

  <table class="noBorder">
  <tr>
    <td class="cellAlignCenter"><b>Fitting</b>
      <div class="dessin"><img src="images/grenaille.png" width="180" height="200" alt="Fitting module"/></div>
    </td>
    <td>
      <p>Local fitting of primitives (planes, lines, spheres, quadratic height fields) on weighted neighborhoods,
      in arbitrary dimension, and the geometric properties derived from them: normals, projections, Growing Least
      Squares descriptors, curvature tensors, Moving Least Squares surfaces, and their derivatives with respect to the
      scale and to the position. The module is generic and extensible: computations are assembled at compile time,
      and new ones can be added.</p>
      <p><b>Main contributors:</b> <i>Nicolas Mellado, Thibault Lejemble</i></p>
      <p>[\ref fitting "User manual"] [\ref fittingreference "Reference"] [\ref fitting_curvature_page "Curvature estimation"]</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="cellAlignCenter"><b>Spatial Partitioning</b>
      <div class="dessin"><img src="images/spatialpartitioning.svg" width="180" height="200" alt="Spatial Partitioning module"/></div>
    </td>
    <td>
      <p>Spatial data structures for proximity queries: kd-trees, k-nearest neighbor graphs and neighbor graphs,
      with nearest, k-nearest and range queries that can also be performed in GPU kernels.</p>
      <p>Thumbnail provided by Basile Fraboni (<a href="https://github.com/bfraboni/vptree-draw" target="_blank">VPTree-Draw</a>).</p>
      <p><b>Main contributors:</b> <i>Thibault Lejemble, Gaël Guennebaud, Nicolas Mellado, Matthieu Gomiero, Dorian Ferreira, Sacha Vincent</i></p>
      <p>[\ref spatialpartitioning "User manual"]</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="cellAlignCenter"><b>Common</b>
      <div class="dessin"><img src="images/common.png" width="180" height="200" alt="Common module"/></div>
    </td>
    <td>
      <p>General purpose classes and methods used by the other modules: point types, point generation, containers
      usable on GPUs, multi-architecture macros and assertions.</p>
      <p>Thumbnail provided by Basile Fraboni (<a href="https://github.com/bfraboni/vptree-draw" target="_blank">VPTree-Draw</a>).</p>
      <p><b>Main contributors:</b> <i>Thibault Lejemble, Gaël Guennebaud</i></p>
      <p>[\ref common "User manual"]</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="cellAlignCenter"><b>PyPonca</b></td>
    <td>
      <p>A Python binding of %Ponca, compatible with NumPy, PyTorch, CuPy and any library supporting DLPack, running on
      the CPU or on CUDA devices.</p>
      <p>[\ref pyponca "User manual"]</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  </table>

  \section user_manual_contents Contents

  The manual is organized as follows:
  - \subpage ponca_points &mdash; how %Ponca accesses your data, and the point types it provides. **Read this first.**
  - \subpage fitting &mdash; fitting primitives on neighborhoods, reading the results, MLS projection, derivatives, and
    the pages dedicated to curvature estimation and to the definition of new computations.
  - \subpage fittingreference &mdash; the list of fitting procedures, capabilities, filters, kernels and status codes.
  - \subpage spatialpartitioning &mdash; kd-trees, neighbor graphs and queries.
  - \subpage common &mdash; point types, containers, macros and assertions shared by all the modules.
  - \subpage ponca_gpu_page &mdash; running %Ponca in CUDA and SYCL kernels.
  - \subpage ponca_concepts &mdash; the requirements on the types given to %Ponca, and the C++20 concepts used by the
    library.
  - \subpage pyponca &mdash; the Python binding.

  \section user_manual_paths Where to start

  - *Estimating normals or curvatures of a point cloud*: \ref ponca_points, then \ref fitting_firstSteps,
    \ref spatialpartitioning_kdtree_usage and \ref fitting_curvature_page.
  - *Projecting points onto a smooth surface defined by a point cloud*: \ref fitting_firstSteps, then
    \ref evaluation_schemes.
  - *Multi-scale analysis*: \ref fitting_neighborhoods_scale and \ref fitting_derivatives.
  - *Integrating %Ponca in an existing code base without copying data*: \ref ponca_points_binding.
  - *Implementing a new method*: \ref fitting_extending_page and \ref ponca_concepts.
 */
} // namespace Ponca
